-- YMD: the ISO 8601-ordered date constructor.
--
-- `YMD y m d` is the recommended constructor for new code. It delegates to
-- `Date d m y`, so the two agree by construction; the only difference is
-- argument order, which YMD makes big-endian to match how ISO 8601 writes a
-- date. `Date d m y` is unchanged and stays supported for existing corpora.
--
-- Harness clock: JL4_FIXED_NOW = 2025-01-31T15:45:30Z.

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

TIMEZONE IS "Etc/UTC"


§ `YMD agrees with Date`

-- expected: DATE OF 28, 7, 2026 — big-endian arguments, little-endian display
#EVAL YMD 2026 7 28

-- expected: TRUE — same date, two argument orders
#EVAL YMD 2026 7 28 EQUALS Date 28 7 2026

-- expected: TRUE — the AKA resolves to the same constructor
#EVAL `Year month day` 2026 7 28 EQUALS YMD 2026 7 28


§ `DATE_SERIAL round-trip`

-- expected: 740189 — days since 1st January 0000
#EVAL DATE_SERIAL (YMD 2026 7 28)

-- expected: DATE OF 28, 7, 2026 — serial round-trips back to the same date
#EVAL Date (DATE_SERIAL (YMD 2026 7 28))

-- expected: TRUE
#EVAL Date (DATE_SERIAL (YMD 2026 7 28)) EQUALS YMD 2026 7 28


§ `The transposition footgun YMD exists to avoid`

-- Transposing month and day is the mistake little-endian `Date d m y` invites,
-- and the failure is SILENT: month 28 is not rejected, it rolls forward.
--
-- expected: DATE OF 28, 7, 2026 — what the author meant
#EVAL YMD 2026 7 28

-- expected: DATE OF 7, 4, 2028 — `Date 7 28 2026` read as day 7, month 28,
-- year 2026; month 28 overflows 2026 by 16 months into April 2028. No error,
-- no warning, just a date two years off.
#EVAL Date 7 28 2026

-- expected: FALSE — the transposed form is a different date entirely
#EVAL Date 7 28 2026 EQUALS YMD 2026 7 28

-- expected: 740808 — 619 days adrift of the intended 740189
#EVAL DATE_SERIAL (Date 7 28 2026)

-- YMD inherits exactly this overflow behaviour, because it delegates: the
-- point is not that YMD validates, it is that YMD is harder to transpose.
-- expected: DATE OF 7, 4, 2028 — YMD 2026 28 7 overflows identically
#EVAL YMD 2026 28 7

-- expected: TRUE — identical by construction
#EVAL YMD 2026 28 7 EQUALS Date 7 28 2026


§ `YMD under a rule-version axis`

-- GST rose from 7% to 9% for rules effective on/after 2024-01-01, expressed
-- with YMD throughout.
`GST rate` MEANS
  IF (DATE_SERIAL `RULES EFFECTIVE DATE`) AT LEAST (DATE_SERIAL (YMD 2024 1 1))
  THEN 9
  ELSE 7

-- expected: 7 — old rule-version
#EVAL `EVAL UNDER RULES EFFECTIVE AT` (DATE_SERIAL (YMD 2023 6 1)) `GST rate`

-- expected: 9 — new rule-version
#EVAL `EVAL UNDER RULES EFFECTIVE AT` (DATE_SERIAL (YMD 2024 7 1)) `GST rate`

-- expected: 9 — axis unset, fallback = localized today 2025-01-31
#EVAL `GST rate`
